WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:02.002
I always go, "When
you make a movie with me,

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:03.587
just put your head down
and do the work."

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.589
People will nit-pick.
They won't pay attention.

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:06.507
They'll cut your budget.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.300
They will not assume
you know what you're doing.

00:00:08.383 --> 00:00:09.718
And then,
if the film's a success,

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:11.553
they always ask,
"How did that happen?"

00:00:11.637 --> 00:00:15.516
I'm like, "I… I don't know.
I honestly don't know."

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:18.852
Sandra Bullock may not know
how the magic happens,

00:00:18.936 --> 00:00:20.646
but there's no denying,
it does.

00:00:20.729 --> 00:00:21.688
From a bus that can't stop

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:24.024
to a space shuttle
that won't start,

00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:26.985
Sandra has spent 30 years
winning over audiences

00:00:27.069 --> 00:00:29.029
with an eclectic mix
of action thrills,

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:31.657
goofy comedies,
and hard-hitting dramas.

00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:38.747
Early TV roles
included a sitcom adaptation

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.416
of the film
Working Girl in 1990

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.377
and put Sandra
on Hollywood's radar,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.213
leading to her role as
scientist turned man magnet

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:49.174
in 1992's Love Potion No. 9

00:00:49.258 --> 00:00:51.260
Before going head-to-head
with Sly Stallone

00:00:51.343 --> 00:00:54.012
in 1993's Demolition Man.

00:00:54.096 --> 00:00:55.639
Now all restaurants
are Taco Bell.

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.017
No way.

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:02.479
Sandra's career
accelerated in 1994,

00:01:02.563 --> 00:01:03.772
quite literally so,

00:01:03.856 --> 00:01:05.232
thanks to a bus
that would explode

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:07.442
if it drops below
50 miles per hour.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.361
Directed by Jan de Bont,

00:01:09.444 --> 00:01:11.196
Speed was
a box office smash.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.575
But did Sandra see this
monster success coming?

00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:15.784
No.

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:18.537
You could sense the snicker

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.123
when people were like,
"Oh, so you're doing Speed.

00:01:21.206 --> 00:01:23.542
You know, it's the bomb
on the bus movie."

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.085
Look, I was ecstatic
to be there.

00:01:25.169 --> 00:01:27.921
I was choice number 74
on the list for the studio.

00:01:28.005 --> 00:01:29.256
Some women turned it down

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:31.842
and Jan de Bont
did a little fighting for me

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.928
and I got to be the Annie
in the driver's seat.

00:01:35.012 --> 00:01:37.347
Yeah, the concept seemed

00:01:37.431 --> 00:01:38.599
quite far-fetched
at the time,

00:01:38.682 --> 00:01:41.226
but when you look
at how realistic

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.477
the people on the bus were,

00:01:42.561 --> 00:01:45.731
and how authentic it felt
to LA, and how diverse,

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:47.649
and how simply
it was executed,

00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:49.902
but, you know, not in
the complexity of the shots,

00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:52.738
but it's a simple premise,
it just struck a chord.

00:01:53.447 --> 00:01:54.656
Miss, can you handle
this bus?

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.742
Oh, sure. It's just like
driving a really big Pinto.

00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:58.911
And look, it didn't do
well because of me.

00:01:58.994 --> 00:02:01.371
It did well because
of divine Keanu Reeves,

00:02:01.455 --> 00:02:03.790
and a great concept
by Jan de Bont,

00:02:03.874 --> 00:02:06.919
that I got to ride along
with and benefit from

00:02:07.002 --> 00:02:08.712
when it had success,
you know.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.380
Hitting the brakes in 1995,

00:02:10.464 --> 00:02:12.174
she starred
in While You Were Sleeping

00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:13.967
as a lonely
train-fare collector

00:02:14.051 --> 00:02:15.886
posing as the girlfriend
of a commuter

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.429
after he falls into a coma.

00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:19.515
It's less sinister
than that sounds.

00:02:19.598 --> 00:02:20.849
It's a romantic
comedy classic

00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:23.602
and earned Sandra
a Golden Globe nomination

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:26.104
for Best Actress
in a Comedy or Musical.

00:02:26.188 --> 00:02:27.689
You know where it's parked,
don't you?

00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:28.899
Oh, yeah. I… I'm…

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:30.567
Yeah.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.949
Expect fewer laughs
in 1995's The Net,

00:02:37.032 --> 00:02:38.492
where Sandra's
computer programmer

00:02:38.575 --> 00:02:40.619
tries to prevent her life
being erased

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:42.412
in a shady conspiracy.

00:02:42.496 --> 00:02:44.665
This more serious side
carried on

00:02:44.748 --> 00:02:47.918
into 1996 wartime weepy
In Love and War,

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.086
where Sandra's nurse

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:51.463
fell for Chris O'Donnell's
Ernest Hemingway.

00:02:51.547 --> 00:02:53.090
I can't stop thinking
about him.

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:54.508
And then,
the blockbuster adaptation

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:56.343
of John Grisham's
A Time to Kill,

00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:58.303
where her idealistic
law student

00:02:58.387 --> 00:03:00.055
teams up
with Matthew McConaughey

00:03:00.138 --> 00:03:02.558
to dish out some
Deep South justice.

00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:05.602
Romance was in the air
in the late 90s

00:03:05.686 --> 00:03:07.813
as Sandra played a jilted
wife finding new love

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.315
in 1998's Hope Floats,

00:03:10.399 --> 00:03:12.734
a witch trying to break
a husband-killing curse

00:03:12.818 --> 00:03:13.861
in Practical Magic,

00:03:13.944 --> 00:03:15.612
and then whisked
Ben Affleck off his feet

00:03:15.696 --> 00:03:18.073
in 1999's Forces of Nature.

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:19.783
I'm… I'm a rebel.

00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:22.035
Little jumpy for a rebel,
aren't you?

00:03:22.119 --> 00:03:25.581
In 2000 film 28 Days,
Sandra went to rehab,

00:03:25.664 --> 00:03:27.082
but it was another film
that year

00:03:27.165 --> 00:03:28.750
that would deliver
an iconic role

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:31.253
as detective turned
beauty queen Grace Hart

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:32.963
in Miss Congeniality.

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:34.798
But just remember to sing.

00:03:34.882 --> 00:03:36.800
S. I. N. G.

00:03:40.846 --> 00:03:42.598
Solar plexus, instep,

00:03:42.681 --> 00:03:44.266
nose, groin.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.435
So much of it is a blur,

00:03:46.518 --> 00:03:48.687
because I didn't
have my family then.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.272
Work was my family.

00:03:50.355 --> 00:03:52.983
I was a working actor,
New York City worker.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:54.818
You take anything
that comes your way

00:03:54.902 --> 00:03:57.196
and you just
make the best of it.

00:03:57.279 --> 00:03:58.697
Miss Congeniality
was sort of

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:00.032
my first big producing job.

00:04:00.115 --> 00:04:01.533
I'd done other smaller ones,

00:04:01.617 --> 00:04:02.659
but I had this one
by the reins.

00:04:02.743 --> 00:04:04.161
I remember
I was making 28 Days,

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:05.579
we were making script notes,

00:04:05.662 --> 00:04:07.372
we were going through
and "Who do we hire?"

00:04:07.456 --> 00:04:09.791
And with Mark Lawrence,
"Where are we gonna shoot?"

00:04:09.875 --> 00:04:11.043
"Let's shoot in Austin,
Texas,

00:04:11.126 --> 00:04:12.503
where it's 120 degrees

00:04:12.586 --> 00:04:13.879
and we're in
a pageant outfit

00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:15.005
with a beehive on our head."

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:16.215
And "What are we doing?"

00:04:16.298 --> 00:04:19.384
But it just felt right
to make that film

00:04:19.468 --> 00:04:23.138
for those girls that didn't
have the ingénue in them

00:04:23.222 --> 00:04:24.973
like I didn't have
the ingénue in me

00:04:25.057 --> 00:04:26.433
when I was looking at films.

00:04:26.517 --> 00:04:28.477
You know, I was
basically playing myself

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.313
and who I wished had been
on screen for me at 14.

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:33.815
It's my daughter's favourite
film, annoyingly so.

00:04:33.899 --> 00:04:35.943
The stuff that she repeats,
I'm like, "Oh, my God.

00:04:36.026 --> 00:04:37.903
I need to apologise
to so many parents

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:39.279
'cause now
I'm getting it back."

00:04:39.363 --> 00:04:40.948
But it's physical comedy,

00:04:41.031 --> 00:04:43.575
and she's a tomboy,
and my daughter is that.

00:04:43.659 --> 00:04:45.410
Definitely
don't show your kids

00:04:45.494 --> 00:04:49.414
2002's Murder by Numbers,
a grim game of cat and mouse

00:04:49.498 --> 00:04:53.585
between Sandra's detective
and two teenage murderers.

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:55.420
Though you are probably
safe to show them

00:04:55.504 --> 00:04:57.506
her delightful double act
with Hugh Grant

00:04:57.589 --> 00:04:58.882
in Two Weeks Notice.

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:01.552
George, I think you're
the most selfish human being

00:05:01.635 --> 00:05:02.511
on the planet,

00:05:02.594 --> 00:05:03.470
That's just silly.

00:05:03.554 --> 00:05:04.721
Have you met everyone
on the planet?

00:05:04.805 --> 00:05:06.557
In 2004,
she starred in Crash,

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.768
a searing look at race
relations in Los Angeles

00:05:09.852 --> 00:05:11.979
that won Best Picture
at that year's Oscars.

00:05:12.062 --> 00:05:13.772
And if that sounds
too intense,

00:05:13.856 --> 00:05:16.900
you only had to wait until
2006 for The Lake House,

00:05:16.984 --> 00:05:19.278
a remake of classic
Korean romance Il Mare

00:05:19.361 --> 00:05:23.031
that reunited her with Speed
co-star Keanu Reeves.

00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:24.449
And it's this versatility

00:05:24.533 --> 00:05:26.743
that has defined
her choice of roles.

00:05:26.827 --> 00:05:30.372
I think back on my dreaming
of what I wanted to play.

00:05:30.455 --> 00:05:31.582
Since a very young age,

00:05:31.665 --> 00:05:33.834
I always wanted the roles
that the men had.

00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:37.462
I would never imagine myself
as the beautiful blonde,

00:05:37.546 --> 00:05:38.547
like the girlfriend,

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:40.340
'cause I was like, "Well,
I don't look like that,

00:05:40.424 --> 00:05:41.967
so I'm not gonna
get those roles."

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.761
What I was drawn to was
the energy, and the fire,

00:05:44.845 --> 00:05:46.930
and the comedy,
and physical comedy,

00:05:47.014 --> 00:05:48.432
and the action,
and the drama

00:05:48.515 --> 00:05:50.851
that the men got to do,
the substance.

00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:54.730
I didn't see myself
as the sexy bombshell,

00:05:54.813 --> 00:05:56.481
which is really
what was being represented

00:05:56.565 --> 00:05:58.859
when I was watching movies
as a kid.

00:05:58.942 --> 00:06:00.277
You definitely see
that substance

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.612
in Sandra's choices.

00:06:01.695 --> 00:06:03.864
In 2006,
she completely transformed

00:06:03.947 --> 00:06:06.408
into To Kill a Mockingbird
author Harper Lee

00:06:06.491 --> 00:06:09.161
in the Truman Capote
biopic Infamous.

00:06:09.244 --> 00:06:10.787
And in 2009,

00:06:10.871 --> 00:06:13.248
she won Best Actress
at the Academy Awards

00:06:13.332 --> 00:06:14.750
for her incredible turn

00:06:14.833 --> 00:06:17.211
as ultimate soccer mom
Leigh Anne Tuohy

00:06:17.294 --> 00:06:19.671
in the NFL drama
The Blind Side.

00:06:21.131 --> 00:06:22.758
Yes! Michael!

00:06:22.841 --> 00:06:24.343
How do you follow an Oscar?

00:06:24.426 --> 00:06:27.596
By making two
of the best films of 2013.

00:06:27.679 --> 00:06:29.264
She made for
a killer double act

00:06:29.348 --> 00:06:30.474
with Melissa McCarthy

00:06:30.557 --> 00:06:32.601
in the raucous cop
comedy The Heat.

00:06:32.684 --> 00:06:33.810
Are you a nark?

00:06:33.894 --> 00:06:35.020
"A nak"?

00:06:35.103 --> 00:06:36.438
I'm sorry,
I don't know what that is.

00:06:36.522 --> 00:06:38.398
Before taking
a doomed trip to space

00:06:38.482 --> 00:06:39.399
with George Clooney

00:06:39.483 --> 00:06:40.984
in the
technologically-astounding

00:06:41.068 --> 00:06:43.403
survival thriller Gravity.

00:06:43.487 --> 00:06:45.531
Almost single-handedly
holding your attention

00:06:45.614 --> 00:06:48.408
for 90 minutes,
this is astonishing stuff.

00:06:48.492 --> 00:06:50.118
She was nominated
for Best Actress

00:06:50.202 --> 00:06:51.912
at the Oscars
for her efforts.

00:06:51.995 --> 00:06:53.539
Sandra came back
to Earth for 2015's

00:06:53.622 --> 00:06:55.374
Our Brand is Crisis

00:06:55.457 --> 00:06:57.251
as a political consultant
trying to win

00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:59.253
a Bolivian election,
before teaming up

00:06:59.336 --> 00:07:01.088
with a monster cast
of Hollywood talent

00:07:01.171 --> 00:07:03.924
for the all-female reboot
of Ocean's Eleven

00:07:04.007 --> 00:07:06.510
in 2018's Ocean's 8.

00:07:06.593 --> 00:07:08.387
All that time in space
with George Clooney

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:09.930
must have rubbed off on her.

00:07:10.013 --> 00:07:12.266
So go home,
get your affairs in order,

00:07:12.349 --> 00:07:14.268
because tomorrow,
we begin pulling off

00:07:14.351 --> 00:07:17.813
one of the biggest
jewellery heists in history.

00:07:17.896 --> 00:07:22.192
2018 also saw her, or rather
didn't see her, in Bird Box,

00:07:22.276 --> 00:07:23.485
the Netflix phenomenon

00:07:23.569 --> 00:07:25.904
where even looking
at the mysterious entities

00:07:25.988 --> 00:07:28.365
triggers people
to kill themselves.

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:30.158
For an unbelievably
bleak concept,

00:07:30.242 --> 00:07:32.870
it did lead to one of the
goofiest social media trends

00:07:32.953 --> 00:07:35.163
as people took
on the Bird Box challenge

00:07:35.247 --> 00:07:36.707
and tried to complete
everyday tasks

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:38.166
while blindfolded.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:41.962
How many kids were slammed
into walls by their parents?

00:07:42.045 --> 00:07:43.172
And I laughed.

00:07:43.255 --> 00:07:44.798
I laughed so hard,
and I was like,

00:07:44.882 --> 00:07:46.091
"I'm not supposed to laugh.

00:07:46.175 --> 00:07:49.136
That poor child just got
nailed into the wall."

00:07:49.219 --> 00:07:50.470
But it was funny.

00:07:51.263 --> 00:07:52.181
It was funny.

00:07:54.016 --> 00:07:55.893
Which leads us to 2021

00:07:55.976 --> 00:07:57.477
and Sandra's
newest Netflix movie,

00:07:57.561 --> 00:07:58.729
The Unforgivable.

00:07:59.521 --> 00:08:00.731
It's another
hugely impressive

00:08:00.814 --> 00:08:02.065
transformation for the actor

00:08:02.149 --> 00:08:03.442
as she plays Ruth Slater,

00:08:03.525 --> 00:08:05.944
a convicted cop-killer
who leaves prison

00:08:06.028 --> 00:08:08.197
and tries to reunite
with her little sister

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.449
in a world that isn't ready
to forgive her.

00:08:11.283 --> 00:08:13.202
There just felt like
there was a lot on the line

00:08:13.285 --> 00:08:14.536
for this.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:16.371
Having these women's
stories,

00:08:16.455 --> 00:08:18.707
and having these women
open up so honestly,

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.792
and seeing
how their entire lives,

00:08:20.876 --> 00:08:23.086
they didn't have an ounce,

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.172
an ounce of what I had.

00:08:25.255 --> 00:08:26.548
And yet they are still

00:08:26.632 --> 00:08:29.176
in belief that
they can figure it out,

00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:31.428
that they can survive,
that they will make it.

00:08:31.512 --> 00:08:33.764
And when they get outside
of one system

00:08:33.847 --> 00:08:36.058
is a step into a great,
unknown system.

00:08:36.141 --> 00:08:37.059
And I just go,

00:08:37.142 --> 00:08:40.854
"If we don't represent
the humanity in that belief,

00:08:40.938 --> 00:08:42.856
regardless
of what the end result was

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:44.942
of what they did,
then we've failed."

00:08:45.025 --> 00:08:46.527
You don't have to
give me a speech.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.696
I'm looking for Katie.
She's my little sister.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:50.656
I raised her.

00:08:50.739 --> 00:08:52.074
Hey, next time,
don't drag me

00:08:52.157 --> 00:08:53.242
through three bus transfers

00:08:53.325 --> 00:08:54.576
to tell me something
I already know.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.578
I think we all
kind of came at it

00:08:56.662 --> 00:08:58.455
with such a good amount
of fear,

00:08:58.539 --> 00:08:59.957
desire, hopefulness,

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:02.042
but respect
for those stories

00:09:02.125 --> 00:09:03.752
that we were responsible
for carrying,

00:09:03.836 --> 00:09:05.212
while entertaining

00:09:05.295 --> 00:09:06.672
and making this
as accessible

00:09:06.755 --> 00:09:08.465
to anyone who can watch it.

00:09:08.549 --> 00:09:10.467
Sure, I think there's gonna
be a handful of people

00:09:10.551 --> 00:09:11.677
who will look at it and go,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:13.178
"I have no idea
how to relate to this.

00:09:13.262 --> 00:09:14.471
Why would someone do this?"

00:09:14.555 --> 00:09:15.472
And I guarantee you,

00:09:15.556 --> 00:09:17.641
it's someone who was born
with affluence,

00:09:17.724 --> 00:09:19.351
someone who never once

00:09:19.434 --> 00:09:21.270
had to fear
for their freedom.

00:09:21.854 --> 00:09:22.855
Never once.

00:09:24.523 --> 00:09:25.983
And that's OK.

00:09:26.066 --> 00:09:27.651
Maybe they'll learn
something along the way.

00:09:27.734 --> 00:09:29.736
You can watch
The Unforgivable

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.655
for yourself
as it's streaming on Netflix

00:09:31.738 --> 00:09:32.990
now.

